    principle:
      'IRC §1031 defers gain when real property held for investment or business is exchanged for like-kind real property. Deferral is indefinite as long as the chain continues, and if the owner dies holding the final property, §1014 step-up can eliminate the deferred gain for income-tax purposes. That is the "swap till you drop" sequence. It is deferral with an exit at death, not exemption, and the procedural rules are unforgiving.',
    howItWorks: [
      'Relinquished property is sold through a qualified intermediary (QI) who holds the proceeds; the taxpayer never touches the cash (Treas. Reg. §1.1031(k)-1).',
      'Replacement property must be identified within 45 days and acquired within 180 days of the sale (§1031(a)(3)).',
      'Gain is deferred to the extent value and debt are replaced; cash or debt relief not replaced is "boot" and taxable now.',
      'Basis carries over (adjusted for boot and new money), so depreciation continues from the old basis and accumulated depreciation recapture is also deferred.',
      'At death, heirs receive stepped-up basis under §1014, and the deferred gain is never recognised for income tax. The property is still in the estate for estate tax.',
    ],
